/**
 * @module ast
 *
 * The {Node} type `zuri.parse()` returns: a generic representation of
 * one AST node, tagged by `kind` (`'Binary'`, `'If'`, `'Function'`,
 * ...) with a `fields` dict holding whatever that kind carries. There
 * is one `Node` class rather than one class per grammar rule, since
 * the grammar has roughly fifty expression/statement/declaration
 * shapes, and a dedicated class per shape would mean fifty near-
 * identical classes to maintain in lockstep with the parser forever
 * after. A `fields` entry that's itself node-shaped (or a list of
 * node-shaped entries) is already wrapped into a {Node} recursively,
 * so you never need to call `Node.from_dict()` yourself.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * for node in zuri.parse('def add(a, b) { return a + b }') {
 *   echo node.kind
 * }
 * # Function
 * ```
 *
 * ## Comments and doc blocks
 *
 * `parse()`'s whole point, beyond exposing the grammar itself, is that
 * its result is complete enough to rebuild the source file from. Every
 * `#`-comment and doc block comment in the file comes back as its own
 * `'Comment'`/`'DocBlock'`-kind node, sitting exactly where it
 * appeared: as a sibling in the surrounding statement/declaration
 * list, at the top level, inside a `{ }` block, or between two class
 * members.
 *
 * This is a firm limitation, not a rare edge case: a comment written
 * inside an expression is silently dropped, not preserved as a node.
 * That covers a comment between call arguments, inside a list/dict
 * literal, or anywhere else mid-expression. CLAUDE.md's own Zuri style
 * guide never places a comment there in the first place. A comment is
 * always its own whole line, sitting between statements, declarations,
 * or class members, so the parser's expression grammar was
 * deliberately left untouched rather than threading trivia capture
 * through every expression rule for a case real, style-conformant
 * Zuri source never actually produces. Source written against that
 * convention loses nothing; source that puts a comment somewhere that
 * convention doesn't allow will lose that one comment.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import zuri
 *
 * var source = "# a helper function\ndef add(a, b) {\n  return a + b\n}\n"
 * var nodes = zuri.parse(source)
 * echo nodes[0].kind        # Comment
 * echo nodes[0].fields.text # " a helper function"
 * echo nodes[1].kind        # Function
 * ```
 *
 * A class body's fields and methods are two separate lists
 * (`Decl::Class`'s own shape, mirrored here as a `Class` node's
 * `properties`/`methods` fields), so a comment between a field and a
 * method lands in `methods`, whichever list the member right after it
 * belongs to, and a comment between two fields lands in `properties`.
 * See `Node`'s own field-by-field notes below. Code that wants the
 * true interleaved order across both lists, to rebuild a class body
 * exactly as written, should merge `properties ++ methods` and sort by
 * `(line, col)`.
 *
 * @copyright 2026, Richard Ore and Zuri contributors
 */

import _zuri_parse

/**
 * One AST node: an expression, statement, declaration, type hint, or
 * a standalone comment/doc block sitting between two of those.
 *
 * @printable
 */
class Node {

  /**
   * Returns a new instance of a {Node} directly, from already-wrapped
   * field values. Ordinary Zuri code should use `Node.from_dict()` or
   * `zuri.parse()` instead, since this constructor does no wrapping of
   * its own.
   *
   * @param string kind: The node's kind, e.g. `'Binary'`, `'If'`
   * @param ?number line: The node's source line, or `nil` if this kind
   *   of node carries no position (see `from_dict`'s own docs)
   * @param ?number col: The node's source column, or `nil`
   * @param dict fields: This kind's own fields, already wrapped
   */
  @new(kind, line, col, fields) {
    self.kind = kind
    self.line = line
    self.col = col
    self.fields = fields
  }

  /**
   * Builds a {Node} from the raw dict `zuri.parse()`'s native backing
   * produces (`{ kind, line, col, fields }`), recursively wrapping any
   * node-shaped entry inside `fields`, a single nested node or a list
   * of them, into its own {Node}. A `fields` entry that isn't
   * node-shaped (a string, a number, a bool, `nil`, or a plain dict
   * like a `Var` node's `type_hint` when there isn't one) passes
   * through unchanged.
   *
   * `line`/`col` are best-effort, not guaranteed on every node: most
   * AST shapes carry a position (an identifier, an operator, a
   * declaration's own name), but a handful of purely structural nodes
   * (an empty `Block`, `None`) don't, and come back with `line`/`col`
   * both `nil`. This never affects what `fields` contains, since
   * position is a convenience for pointing at where something is, not
   * part of the information rebuilding source needs.
   *
   * @param dict data: `{ kind, line, col, fields }`
   * @returns Node
   */
  static from_dict(data) {
    var fields = {}
    for key in data.fields.keys() {
      fields[key] = _wrap_node(data.fields[key])
    }
    return Node(data.kind, data.line, data.col, fields)
  }

  /**
   * Is this node a standalone comment or doc block (as opposed to a
   * real expression/statement/declaration)? See this module's own doc
   * comment for exactly where these can and can't appear.
   *
   * @returns bool
   */
  is_trivia() {
    return self.kind == 'Comment' or self.kind == 'DocBlock'
  }

  /**
   * This node as `kind@line:col`, e.g. `'Binary@3:5'`, or just `kind`
   * when it carries no position.
   * @returns string
   */
  to_string() {
    if self.line == nil {
      return self.kind
    }
    return '${self.kind}@${self.line}:${self.col}'
  }

  @to_string() {
    return self.to_string()
  }
}

/**
 * Recursively wraps a raw value from a node's `fields` dict: a
 * node-shaped dict becomes a {Node}, a list has this applied to each
 * of its own entries, and everything else (a string, number, bool,
 * `nil`, or a plain non-node dict) passes through as-is.
 *
 * A dict counts as "node-shaped" when it carries both a `kind` and a
 * `fields` entry, the two keys nothing else `zuri.parse()` produces
 * happens to have together, so this can't misfire on a `TypeHint`'s
 * plain `{ types, nullable }` fields dict or similar.
 */
def _wrap_node(value) {
  if is_list(value) {
    var result = []
    for item in value {
      result.append(_wrap_node(item))
    }
    return result
  }

  if is_dict(value) and value.get('kind', nil) != nil and value.get('fields', nil) != nil {
    return Node.from_dict(value)
  }

  return value
}

/**
 * Parses `source` in full and returns its top-level declarations as a
 * list of {Node}. See this module's own doc comment for the exact
 * shape, and in particular for what is and isn't preserved around
 * comments.
 *
 * Raises (rather than returning a partial result) on a syntax error,
 * since there is no meaningful AST to hand back for source the parser
 * itself couldn't make sense of.
 *
 * @param string source: The Zuri source to parse
 * @returns list[Node]
 * @throws Error if `source` has a syntax error
 */
def parse(source) {
  var nodes = []
  for raw in _zuri_parse.parse(source) {
    nodes.append(Node.from_dict(raw))
  }
  return nodes
}
